{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1427 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b26c4cdba3c683a7be592c8deaf9fcc68b0503508013cd15602027a1190f9cca" :edb/db-before-t 1426 :edb/db-after-t 1427 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534739 50 #inst "2026-10-12T10:03:52.697Z" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087420 1071 "Which of the following is a list containing only prime numbers?\n\n1. $3, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 17$\n2. $3, \\: 7, \\: 11, \\: 29$\n3. $2, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 13$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087420 1071 "Which of the following is a list containing only prime numbers?\n\nI. $3, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 17$\n\nII. $3, \\: 7, \\: 11, \\: 29$\n\nIII. $2, \\: 4, \\: 7, \\: 13$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087420 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in each list.\n\n- List I includes $8$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1$, $2$, $4$, and $8$.\n- List II contains only prime numbers since $3$, $7$, $11$, and $29$ have only two factors each: $1$ and themselves.\n- List III contains $4$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1$, $2$, and $4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087420 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in each list.\n\n- List I includes $8$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors:  $1,$ $2,$ $4,$ and $8.$\n\n- List II contains only prime numbers since $3,$ $7,$ $11,$ and $29$ have only two factors each: $1$ and themselves.\n\n- List III contains $4$, which is not prime as it has more than two factors: $1,$ $2,$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087421 1071 "Which of the following lists contains only composite numbers?\n\n1. $4, \\: 16, \\: 24, \\: 28$\n2. $6, \\: 15, \\: 19, \\: 20$\n3. $8, \\: 15, \\: 21, \\: 29$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087421 1071 "Which of the following lists contains only composite numbers?\n\nI. $4, \\: 16, \\: 24, \\: 28$\n\nII. $6, \\: 15, \\: 19, \\: 20$\n\nIII. $8, \\: 15, \\: 21, \\: 29$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087421 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$. A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each list.\n\n- List I contains only composite numbers. Notice that all of them have $2$ as a factor.\n- List II includes $19$, which is prime.\n- List III contains $29$, which is prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087421 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each list.\n\n- List I contains only composite numbers. Notice that all of them have $2$ as a factor.\n\n- List II includes $19,$ which is prime.\n\n- List III contains $29,$ which is prime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087422 1071 "Which of the following numbers are prime?\n\n$$\n13, \\quad 15, \\quad  17, \\quad  1, \\quad  6\n$$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087422 1071 "Which of the following numbers are prime?\n$13, \\quad 15, \\quad 17, \\quad 1, \\quad 6$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087422 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1$. A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13$.\n- $15$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1$. For example, $3$ is a factor of $15$:\n$15= 3\\times 5$\n- $17$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $17$.\n- $1$ is *not* prime since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* composite either.\n- $6$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1$. For example, $3$ is a factor of $6$:\n$6= 3\\times 2$\n\nTherefore, only $13$ and $17$ are prime." 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186087422 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$ A composite number is a number that has more than two distinct factors.\n\nThe number $1$ is neither prime nor composite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the numbers in the list.\n\n- $13$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $13.$\n\n- $15$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $15\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n15= 3\\times 5\n$$\n\n- $17$ is prime since it has only two factors: $1$ and $17.$\n\n- $1$ is *not* prime since it has only one factor. Moreover, $1$ is *not* composite either.\n\n- $6$ is *not* prime as it has factors distinct from itself and $1.$ For example, $3$ is a factor of $6\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n6= 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $13$ and $17$ are prime." 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434654 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434655 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434651 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434653 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434650 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434652 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186087912 1050 420.0 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186088985 1064 "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder.\n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1$.\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186088985 1064 "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder. \n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1.$\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186088986 1064 "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**.\n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2$, and $4$.\n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$\n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1$." 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186088986 1064 "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**. \n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2,$ and $4.$ \n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$ \n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1.$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186088987 1064 "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both.\n\nThe only exception is the number $1$. The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1$." 13194139534739 17592186045417 false] [17592186088987 1064 "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both. \n\nThe only exception is the number $1.$ The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1.$" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434614 1080 "$13$ and $23$ only" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434614 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434614 1083 #uuid "0170376b-1703-5848-91b7-1bdf5372127a" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434615 1080 "32" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434615 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434615 1083 #uuid "04ba9281-e055-5aba-970f-eea79fd99e1b" 13194139534739 17592186045417 true] [17592186434616 1080 "3" 1319